From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 13 Aug 2026 20:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Tell the guest where Emu68's own memory ends

Emu68 carves its allocator and its JIT buffers out of the same DRAM it then
advertises to the guest as system memory, and says nothing about it. A guest
that builds its heap from the advertised range therefore hands out addresses
Emu68 is already using, and the two write over each other.

Measured on this port, 2026-08-13. Emu68's SYS pool ran 0x0088a000-0x01ffffff
while the AROS heap started at 0x01000000: sixteen megabytes with two
independent allocators handing out the same addresses. It surfaced as
free-list corruption inside the guest's allocator -- its free_node pointers
read back as 0x000c2c6f and 0x00104eae, which are not pointers but data, in a
block sitting squarely inside the overlap.

That also explains a boot failure this project spent a long time attributing
to a wild pointer in the guest. There was no use-after-free: the memory had
another owner the whole time.

So publish the extent as a plain [start, end) pair on /emu68, beside the
properties already there. This reserves nothing and decides nothing -- it
states a fact the guest has no other way to discover, and leaves what to do
about it to the guest.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
Written beside vc4-mem, with the rest of the memory description. A first
attempt put it next to the pool sizes printed later in boot, which is after
the tree the guest receives has been settled: the property never arrived.

---
--- a/src/aarch64/start.c
+++ b/src/aarch64/start.c
@@ -1372,6 +1372,36 @@
             }
         }
 
+        /*
+            Tell the guest where our own memory ends.
+
+            Emu68's allocator and its JIT buffers are carved out of the same
+            DRAM the guest is then told it owns, and nothing has ever said so.
+            A guest that builds its heap from the advertised range hands out
+            addresses this allocator is already using, and the two write over
+            each other -- measured on 2026-08-13 as free-list corruption inside
+            the guest's own allocator, whose free_node pointers came back
+            holding data rather than addresses.
+
+            A plain [start, end) pair on /emu68, beside vc4-mem. It has to be
+            written here, with the rest of the memory description: a first
+            attempt put it next to the pool sizes printed later in boot, which
+            is after the tree the guest receives has been settled, and the
+            property never reached it.
+
+            This reserves nothing and decides nothing. It states a fact the
+            guest has no other way to discover and leaves the policy to it.
+        */
+        {
+            uint32_t hostmem[2] = {
+                (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)&__bootstrap_end,
+                (uint32_t)(((uintptr_t)boot + (KERNEL_SYS_PAGES << 21))
+                           & ~((1 << 21) - 1))
+            };
+            dt_add_property(dt_find_node("/emu68"), "host-mem", hostmem,
+                            sizeof(hostmem));
+        }
+
         /* Make a hole for 0xdeadbeef */
         mmu_map(0xdeadbeef & ~4095, 0xdeadbeef & ~4095, 4096, 0, 0);
 
